WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.763
Welcome to Part 1 of the implementation
series for HR-driven identity.

2
00:00:04.763 --> 00:00:07.401
The HR pipeline makes an account exist.

3
00:00:07.401 --> 00:00:10.462
This guide makes the HR
record decide everything that

4
00:00:10.462 --> 00:00:13.387
happens to that account
afterward, and proves it did.

5
00:00:13.387 --> 00:00:15.112
It does that in seven gates.

6
00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:18.680
For each gate, we'll cover
what done means, the steps,

7
00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:22.509
the commands from the
developer workbook's first lab,

8
00:00:22.509 --> 00:00:26.511
the federal requirement and
the evidence an ISSO keeps,

9
00:00:26.511 --> 00:00:30.601
what's available in GCC and GCC
High, and how to roll it back.

10
00:00:30.601 --> 00:00:35.007
Part 1 takes the first three gates:
one writer for every account state,

11
00:00:35.007 --> 00:00:39.800
employees and contractors from their
sources, and joiners ready on day one.

12
00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:41.675
Part 2 takes the other four.

13
00:00:41.675 --> 00:00:45.825
Design guidance is labeled, and the
workbook's code is illustrative,

14
00:00:45.825 --> 00:00:49.310
tested against fixtures
rather than a live tenant.

15
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.297
Here are all seven gates on one page.

16
00:00:55.297 --> 00:00:59.236
The first is done when every
account state and HR attribute has

17
00:00:59.236 --> 00:01:03.173
exactly one recorded writer, and
the other write paths are gone.

18
00:01:03.173 --> 00:01:05.894
The second is done when
employees come from HR,

19
00:01:05.894 --> 00:01:09.834
and contractors come from a named
source with an enforced end date.

20
00:01:09.834 --> 00:01:14.311
The third is done when the pre-hire
and day-one workflows run with limits,

21
00:01:14.311 --> 00:01:16.435
and day-one readiness is measured.

22
00:01:16.435 --> 00:01:20.391
Part 2 picks up from there:
access assigned from attributes,

23
00:01:20.391 --> 00:01:23.136
with a review of what
a move leaves behind;

24
00:01:23.136 --> 00:01:28.384
PIV and CAC credentials tied to HR events;
separations that block access first

25
00:01:28.384 --> 00:01:33.147
and dispose of the account last; and
drift found and closed at the source.

26
00:01:33.147 --> 00:01:36.914
Each gate names the validation
kit checks that prove part of it,

27
00:01:36.914 --> 00:01:39.448
and says plainly where
no check exists yet.

28
00:01:39.448 --> 00:01:45.100
Every threshold is a proposed starting
point the agency approves or replaces.

29
00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.375
The gates assume some things
are already in place.

30
00:01:51.375 --> 00:01:52.988
The HR pipeline is live,

31
00:01:52.988 --> 00:01:57.487
with shadow differences explained
and no unmatched creations.

32
00:01:57.487 --> 00:02:01.321
The attribute foundation is done:
the slot register is signed,

33
00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:03.951
and the attributes are
proven on the pilot.

34
00:02:03.951 --> 00:02:09.055
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
03's first-phase decisions are made:

35
00:02:09.055 --> 00:02:13.688
the person key, the authority map, the
worker types and the privacy basis.

36
00:02:13.688 --> 00:02:15.087
Licensing matters.

37
00:02:15.087 --> 00:02:20.585
Inbound provisioning needs Entra ID P1,
but workflows, entitlement management,

38
00:02:20.585 --> 00:02:25.250
access reviews and automatic
assignment need Entra ID Governance.

39
00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:29.987
Roles go through PIM, with the workflows
administrator role behind approval.

40
00:02:29.987 --> 00:02:32.929
And logs leave Entra ID
before the first gate,

41
00:02:32.929 --> 00:02:37.063
because its own retention is short
and can't be recovered later.

42
00:02:37.063 --> 00:02:41.765
As for where things run: the HR platform
runs on Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,

43
00:02:41.765 --> 00:02:44.526
and the lifecycle's
automation runs in Azure.

44
00:02:44.526 --> 00:02:47.523
Apps on AWS or Oracle Cloud only see these

45
00:02:47.523 --> 00:02:51.400
changes through the access
Entra ID grants them.

46
00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:57.138
The first gate starts from one idea.

47
00:02:57.138 --> 00:02:59.906
Whoever can write an attribute effectively

48
00:02:59.906 --> 00:03:02.675
decides any access that
a rule grants from it.

49
00:03:02.675 --> 00:03:06.417
Account state works the same
way, and two systems can set it:

50
00:03:06.417 --> 00:03:08.912
the pipeline, and Lifecycle Workflows.

51
00:03:08.912 --> 00:03:12.025
So the register gets a
row for every transition,

52
00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:14.500
each with one writer and one backstop.

53
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:18.300
Inbound provisioning creates the
account, and nothing else does.

54
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:21.250
At hire, pick one writer
to enable the account,

55
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:26.513
either the pipeline or the day-one workflow,
and record the other as the backstop.

56
00:03:26.513 --> 00:03:29.133
At separation, the pipeline disables the

57
00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:32.138
account before the leaver
workflow first runs.

58
00:03:32.138 --> 00:03:35.676
Only the pipeline can re-enable
a cancelled separation.

59
00:03:35.676 --> 00:03:40.675
Deletion belongs to a separate post-separation
workflow, never to a bulk upload.

60
00:03:40.675 --> 00:03:45.600
The hire and leave dates, and the manager,
are written only by provisioning.

61
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.238
HR, not IT, records a new supervisor.

62
00:03:49.238 --> 00:03:52.620
And the certificate binding
belongs to the card system.

63
00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.595
Three more steps finish the first gate.

64
00:03:58.595 --> 00:04:02.749
First, write down which records
provision at all, including leave,

65
00:04:02.749 --> 00:04:06.595
secondments and pre-hire lookahead,
and version that filter.

66
00:04:06.595 --> 00:04:09.095
Remember, someone on leave hasn't left.

67
00:04:09.095 --> 00:04:14.108
Second, remove every other way to write
these values: the help desk, self-service,

68
00:04:14.108 --> 00:04:15.805
Exchange administrators,

69
00:04:15.805 --> 00:04:19.970
and the permissions on Active
Directory's organizational units.

70
00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:21.833
One path is easy to miss.

71
00:04:21.833 --> 00:04:25.260
Whoever can write the
certificate mapping attribute in

72
00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.470
AD decides which card can
sign in as which synced user.

73
00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.885
Third, save an audit query
for each protected value,

74
00:04:31.885 --> 00:04:35.382
one that finds writes by
anyone but the named writer.

75
00:04:35.382 --> 00:04:37.594
Gate seven will run those queries.

76
00:04:37.594 --> 00:04:40.320
The interim path matters
for most agencies.

77
00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.045
Until AD retires, sync is a writer too,
so record the sync agent's managed

78
00:04:45.045 --> 00:04:50.280
service account as a writer, and review
those permissions every quarter.

79
00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:57.537
The workbook's first chapter
turns the register into code,

80
00:04:57.537 --> 00:05:01.043
and its lab runs on fictional
audit records with no tenant.

81
00:05:01.043 --> 00:05:04.413
The check reads the register
itself: one writer per row,

82
00:05:04.413 --> 00:05:08.383
every required transition present,
no deletes by provisioning,

83
00:05:08.383 --> 00:05:11.454
and the register signed
with its scope versioned.

84
00:05:11.454 --> 00:05:14.055
The program's own register
comes back clean.

85
00:05:14.055 --> 00:05:17.693
Then the scan compares a month of
audit records with the register.

86
00:05:17.693 --> 00:05:19.867
It finds eleven protected writes.

87
00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:22.920
Five came from the right
writer, one from the backstop,

88
00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:26.805
four from a second writer, and one
from a writer the register forbids.

89
00:05:26.805 --> 00:05:29.389
So a help desk account disabled a leaver,

90
00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:33.930
a fix-up script wrote a coded slot,
and provisioning deleted an account.

91
00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:35.180
Two limits apply.

92
00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:37.680
The scan doesn't read Active Directory.

93
00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:41.799
And the audit property names it
matches aren't documented by Microsoft,

94
00:05:41.799 --> 00:05:45.450
so confirm them in a test tenant
before you trust the scan.

95
00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.437
Here's the federal side of the first gate.

96
00:05:51.437 --> 00:05:56.188
Account management and identifier management
are both in the Moderate baseline.

97
00:05:56.188 --> 00:05:59.319
For those, the ISSO keeps
the signed register,

98
00:05:59.319 --> 00:06:03.212
with its lifecycle rows and
its versioned scope filter.

99
00:06:03.212 --> 00:06:06.864
Audit review covers writes
made outside the named writer,

100
00:06:06.864 --> 00:06:11.124
and the evidence is the saved queries
plus the AD auditing settings.

101
00:06:11.124 --> 00:06:14.763
The feed also needs a privacy
determination for each flow.

102
00:06:14.763 --> 00:06:17.575
To validate, two kit checks
confirm there are no

103
00:06:17.575 --> 00:06:20.675
duplicate keys and that the
key fields are filled in.

104
00:06:20.675 --> 00:06:23.113
But they measure values, not writers.

105
00:06:23.113 --> 00:06:25.449
Nothing in the kit
reads who can write yet.

106
00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:29.944
So the evidence for writers is the
delegation export, the permission review,

107
00:06:29.944 --> 00:06:33.938
and a clean run of the saved queries
over the pilot's last thirty days.

108
00:06:33.938 --> 00:06:38.151
Everything this gate uses is
available in both government clouds.

109
00:06:38.151 --> 00:06:40.938
To roll back, make a
signed register change.

110
00:06:40.938 --> 00:06:46.110
A removed permission comes back only
through a recorded change, with an end date.

111
00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:53.548
The second gate brings each
population in from its own source.

112
00:06:53.548 --> 00:06:56.622
For employees, the
pipeline already exists.

113
00:06:56.622 --> 00:07:01.151
This gate checks one thing: that the feed
reads hires and separations by their

114
00:07:01.151 --> 00:07:05.610
published date, so the pre-hire and
pre-separation workflows have time to run.

115
00:07:05.610 --> 00:07:10.147
Contractors are different, because
they aren't in Federal HR 2.0.

116
00:07:10.147 --> 00:07:15.040
The program's decision is an agency-owned
contractor system of record for now,

117
00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.022
with Oracle's contingent
worker as a later swap.

118
00:07:18.022 --> 00:07:19.885
Measure before you change anything.

119
00:07:19.885 --> 00:07:23.801
Reconcile the contractor
register, the card system and the

120
00:07:23.801 --> 00:07:27.797
accounts in both directories,
read-only, using the person key.

121
00:07:27.797 --> 00:07:32.965
Then settle six questions with the
register's owner: who owns it, what's in it,

122
00:07:32.965 --> 00:07:37.285
how fresh it is, its privacy basis,
the join key, and its coverage.

123
00:07:37.285 --> 00:07:41.504
Contractors map into the same record,
with their own worker type code,

124
00:07:41.504 --> 00:07:44.835
the sponsor as manager, and
their own provisioning app.

125
00:07:44.835 --> 00:07:48.410
The contractor PIV path
is still pending proof.

126
00:07:52.160 --> 00:07:55.210
For contractors, ship the
leaver before the joiner.

127
00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:59.660
An end date in a spreadsheet that
nobody updates keeps access alive.

128
00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:02.260
So the middleware works
out the earliest end.

129
00:08:02.260 --> 00:08:07.139
That's the contract end, capped by the
completion date; suitability withdrawn;

130
00:08:07.139 --> 00:08:10.722
the card terminated; the sponsor
gone past a grace period;

131
00:08:10.722 --> 00:08:13.085
or a missed sponsor recertification.

132
00:08:13.085 --> 00:08:15.708
Whichever comes first,
the middleware sends

133
00:08:15.708 --> 00:08:18.473
the leave date and disables
the account itself.

134
00:08:18.473 --> 00:08:20.723
The table comes from the workbook's lab.

135
00:08:20.723 --> 00:08:24.673
Four fictional contractors go
inactive, each for a different reason.

136
00:08:24.673 --> 00:08:27.512
One of them had a card
expiring in late October,

137
00:08:27.512 --> 00:08:31.772
but a recertification that came due
earlier, so the earlier date wins.

138
00:08:31.772 --> 00:08:33.739
Two records are quarantined,

139
00:08:33.739 --> 00:08:37.835
one with no contract end and one
with a sponsor HR doesn't know.

140
00:08:37.835 --> 00:08:41.560
Sponsors recertify through a
twice-yearly access review.

141
00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:46.940
Here's the design choice: if the sponsor
doesn't respond, access is removed.

142
00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:53.944
The workbook's second
chapter builds this in code,

143
00:08:53.944 --> 00:08:57.040
reusing the HR middleware
that already exists.

144
00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:01.632
The extract step takes nine fictional
records and finds three active,

145
00:09:01.632 --> 00:09:04.878
four past their earliest
end, and two quarantined.

146
00:09:04.878 --> 00:09:09.915
Every record carries its end date, so the
leaver workflow fires on the right day.

147
00:09:09.915 --> 00:09:12.632
The middleware turns that extract into one

148
00:09:12.632 --> 00:09:15.727
batch of seven requests,
including four disables.

149
00:09:15.727 --> 00:09:19.152
Nothing is sent, because
a dry run is its default.

150
00:09:19.152 --> 00:09:22.827
Then the read-only reconciliation
finds seven issues.

151
00:09:22.827 --> 00:09:26.221
Four are accounts still enabled
past their earliest end,

152
00:09:26.221 --> 00:09:29.253
and they're exactly the four
disables waiting to go.

153
00:09:29.253 --> 00:09:33.507
The others are an orphan account, a card
for someone the source doesn't know,

154
00:09:33.507 --> 00:09:36.227
and a leave date that would
fire on the wrong day.

155
00:09:36.227 --> 00:09:40.064
Finally, the review step writes
the sponsor review settings.

156
00:09:40.064 --> 00:09:43.853
Once the disables go out, the
next reconciliation is clean.

157
00:09:43.853 --> 00:09:46.960
That clean re-run is the
evidence for the gate.

158
00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:55.679
On the federal side, account management and
external personnel security call for the

159
00:09:55.679 --> 00:09:58.269
source decision, the
reconciliation report,

160
00:09:58.269 --> 00:10:02.398
and how long each contractor's access
took to block after their end date.

161
00:10:02.398 --> 00:10:05.289
Identifying each person
by status means the

162
00:10:05.289 --> 00:10:08.023
worker type code appears on every account.

163
00:10:08.023 --> 00:10:11.923
OMB's zero trust memo says
enterprise identity draws on HR,

164
00:10:11.923 --> 00:10:15.011
contract management
and personnel security,

165
00:10:15.011 --> 00:10:20.048
so the governance body signs a table
that names one authority for each fact.

166
00:10:20.048 --> 00:10:23.776
To validate, one kit check lists
enabled accounts with no key,

167
00:10:23.776 --> 00:10:26.685
which is where contractors
without one show up.

168
00:10:26.685 --> 00:10:31.614
No check reads end dates, so the
evidence is the reconciliation re-run,

169
00:10:31.614 --> 00:10:34.035
with no contractor past their end.

170
00:10:34.035 --> 00:10:36.713
In Azure Government, API-driven inbound

171
00:10:36.713 --> 00:10:39.960
provisioning and access
reviews are available.

172
00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:42.580
Until the contractor source is automated,

173
00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:46.848
keep the contract end date as the
leave date so the leaver still fires.

174
00:10:46.848 --> 00:10:49.473
To roll back, pause the contractor app.

175
00:10:49.473 --> 00:10:51.100
It keeps its state.

176
00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:57.562
The third gate makes
joiners ready on day one.

177
00:10:57.562 --> 00:11:02.138
The account already exists, disabled,
because the pipeline sent it early.

178
00:11:02.138 --> 00:11:05.921
Build just a few workflows:
pre-hire, day-one, mover,

179
00:11:05.921 --> 00:11:10.650
leaver, post-leaver, and the
rehire and conversion workflows.

180
00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:14.612
Scope them by coded attributes,
never one per organization.

181
00:11:14.612 --> 00:11:18.171
Microsoft gives two different
ceilings for workflows,

182
00:11:18.171 --> 00:11:21.962
fifty on one page and a hundred
on another, so budget to fifty.

183
00:11:21.962 --> 00:11:24.726
A workflow can hold up
to twenty-five tasks.

184
00:11:24.726 --> 00:11:27.964
The tenant schedule runs
every three hours by default

185
00:11:27.964 --> 00:11:30.700
and can be set anywhere
from one to twenty-four.

186
00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:34.987
Every workflow shares it, so set
it once, with the leaver in mind.

187
00:11:34.987 --> 00:11:37.288
Set the hire time early in the day.

188
00:11:37.288 --> 00:11:41.124
Give every scheduled workflow
an execution limit a little

189
00:11:41.124 --> 00:11:44.412
above a normal week, so an
anomaly gets quarantined.

190
00:11:44.412 --> 00:11:48.800
In the workbook's files, the day-one
workflow doesn't enable the account.

191
00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.490
The pipeline does.

192
00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:57.590
A new hire needs a way to sign
in before their card arrives.

193
00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:00.909
The pre-hire workflow has a
built-in task that creates

194
00:12:00.909 --> 00:12:04.152
a Temporary Access Pass and
emails it to the manager.

195
00:12:04.152 --> 00:12:05.790
It starts on the hire date.

196
00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:09.127
It needs the manager and the
manager's email filled in.

197
00:12:09.127 --> 00:12:12.701
And it only works for someone
with no other sign-in methods,

198
00:12:12.701 --> 00:12:15.753
no earlier sign-ins and no
Entra role assignments.

199
00:12:15.753 --> 00:12:20.577
So a rehire who kept their old methods
won't qualify, and needs a separate path.

200
00:12:20.577 --> 00:12:24.226
Microsoft recommends a pass
sent to a new hire last no more

201
00:12:24.226 --> 00:12:27.952
than twenty-four hours, and the
tenant can force one-time use.

202
00:12:27.952 --> 00:12:30.909
Day-one access comes from
an onboarding package,

203
00:12:30.909 --> 00:12:34.678
with department access assigned
automatically in gate four.

204
00:12:34.678 --> 00:12:37.802
Never add a new hire straight
to an application group.

205
00:12:37.802 --> 00:12:41.652
Then measure the share of hires who are
fully ready at the start of day one.

206
00:12:41.652 --> 00:12:43.502
The pass is only a bridge.

207
00:12:43.502 --> 00:12:48.390
Once the card is bound, an account
still running on a pass is a finding.

208
00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:56.153
The workbook's third chapter keeps
the workflows as reviewed files.

209
00:12:56.153 --> 00:12:58.864
Its lint passes the program's six files.

210
00:12:58.864 --> 00:13:03.864
Against three broken copies, it catches
an offset outside Microsoft's range,

211
00:13:03.864 --> 00:13:07.832
too many workflows for the
budget, a missing execution limit,

212
00:13:07.832 --> 00:13:10.133
a joiner that adds groups directly,

213
00:13:10.133 --> 00:13:14.815
delete and disable in the same workflow,
and a pass that lasts three days.

214
00:13:14.815 --> 00:13:18.665
The plan step refuses to run
if any file has a finding.

215
00:13:18.665 --> 00:13:21.740
Then it compares each file
with the tenant's export.

216
00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:26.346
A missing workflow becomes a create,
and a changed one becomes a new version,

217
00:13:26.346 --> 00:13:29.615
because workflows are
versioned, not edited in place.

218
00:13:29.615 --> 00:13:32.390
It reads with one permission
and sends nothing.

219
00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:36.927
Execution limits aren't in the
API, so record them in the file.

220
00:13:36.927 --> 00:13:41.241
Last, readiness checks each hire
for an enabled, licensed account,

221
00:13:41.241 --> 00:13:44.677
the onboarding package, and
a completed pre-hire run.

222
00:13:44.677 --> 00:13:47.077
One of four fictional hires is ready.

223
00:13:47.077 --> 00:13:49.800
Run it on an export from
the start of the day.

224
00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:57.871
On the federal side, automated account
management calls for the workflow

225
00:13:57.871 --> 00:13:59.571
definitions, their versions,

226
00:13:59.571 --> 00:14:03.325
and run history exported before
Entra's thirty-day view drops it.

227
00:14:03.325 --> 00:14:06.984
Authenticator management requires
that the first authenticator

228
00:14:06.984 --> 00:14:09.663
is time-limited and goes
to an accountable person.

229
00:14:09.663 --> 00:14:14.051
So the ISSO keeps the pass
policy and the task's settings.

230
00:14:14.051 --> 00:14:16.738
To validate, one kit
check flags hires whose

231
00:14:16.738 --> 00:14:19.725
start date has passed with
no processing record.

232
00:14:19.725 --> 00:14:24.837
Another confirms execution limits are set,
and counts workflows against the ceiling.

233
00:14:24.837 --> 00:14:28.987
Add the run history for the last group
of hires, and the readiness rate.

234
00:14:28.987 --> 00:14:34.075
Lifecycle Workflows and entitlement
management are available in Azure Government.

235
00:14:34.075 --> 00:14:39.024
But Microsoft's government table has
no row for the Temporary Access Pass,

236
00:14:39.024 --> 00:14:42.188
so its availability in
GCC High isn't confirmed.

237
00:14:42.188 --> 00:14:45.701
Before Governance is licensed,
the pipeline enables the

238
00:14:45.701 --> 00:14:49.213
account on the hire date and
the help desk issues the pass.

239
00:14:49.213 --> 00:14:52.500
To roll back, disable
the workflow's schedule.

240
00:14:56.250 --> 00:15:01.150
To close Part 1, here's what each gate
leaves behind, and where Part 2 picks it up.

241
00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:05.937
The first gate leaves the register, the
scope filter and the saved audit queries.

242
00:15:05.937 --> 00:15:10.311
The separation gate relies on its rule
that the pipeline disables first,

243
00:15:10.311 --> 00:15:12.425
and the drift gate runs its queries.

244
00:15:12.425 --> 00:15:15.057
The second gate leaves
the source decisions,

245
00:15:15.057 --> 00:15:18.188
the authority table and
the reconciliation reports.

246
00:15:18.188 --> 00:15:22.263
The credentials gate reads contractor
card events, and the separation

247
00:15:22.263 --> 00:15:26.200
gate measures how fast contractors
are blocked after their end date.

248
00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.475
The third gate leaves the
workflows and their run history.

249
00:15:29.475 --> 00:15:32.364
The mover workflow, the
end of the pass bridge,

250
00:15:32.364 --> 00:15:34.863
and the leaver sequence all build on it.

251
00:15:34.863 --> 00:15:40.925
Part 2 covers access and movers, credentials,
separation, and drift and evidence.

252
00:15:40.925 --> 00:15:44.570
To build Part 1's code,
use the developer workbook,

253
00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:49.763
Building HR-Driven Identity, chapters
one to three, each with its labs.

254
00:15:49.763 --> 00:15:54.394
And to learn it in order, use the
learning path for HR-driven identity:

255
00:15:54.394 --> 00:15:59.340
seven modules, one per gate, each with
a fixture lab and a knowledge check.
